  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nCurrent satellite and radar depict a line of scattered weak \nrain showers/virga decaying over central Lk MI this morning. \nWater vapor satellite shows that this is the leading edge of \nmoisture aloft moving up and over an upper ridge (axis is \ncurrently over eastern MI and Lk Huron). The northern stream \nupper trough in CAN is pushing against this upper ridge, slowly \ntrending heights down over MI this morning. Some weak upper \ndivergence and the washed out/weakening boundary aloft is \nresulting in these weakening showers. Little to no precipitation\nis expected from these as this continues to weaken and move \nacross northern lower this morning. However, cloud cover will \ncontinue to increase as the aforementioned upper trough \ncontinues to break down the upper ridging over MI today. \nAlthough sub-tropical moisture will flow over northern MI today,\nvery little forcing will be present. The upper wave in CAN \ntracks east, which will only assist in 30kts of a southwest LLJ \nover eastern upper and near the tip of the mitt around midday. \nThis paired with a weakening surface boundary will yield areas \nof stratiform light rain starting around mid morning for \neastern upper. Off and on light rain chances linger through much\nof the day as these features are slow to progress and continue \nto weaken throughout the day. Most CAM guidance agrees in a\ncouple tenths of an inch of rain in total across eastern upper\nby late today. A window of weak instability is possible midday,\nand could lead to an embedded shower or two. Low to no chances\nfor thunder. Breezy southwest winds and continued WAA will be \npresent over northern lower, while clouds and a cold front \nslowly creep across eastern upper.. which leads to a 15 degree \ntemperature gradient across the area today. Temperatures will \nremain in the high 50s to low 60s over eastern upper, while \nparts of NE lower see temperatures warm into the 70s thanks to \nsome light down sloping and that WAA. \n\nAs the near surface boundary seeps southward late today into \ntonight, winds will turn north and slight chances for light rain \nwill be seen along it over northern lower. Best chances at this time \nreside along and north of M-32, reaching the southern extent early \nMonday morning. Forecasted rain amounts remain at a couple\nhundredths up to a tenth of an inch at most for northern lower.\nCloud cover persist into Monday with high temperatures \nremaining in the 40s and 50s. A 1035mb surface high will slide \nto the east over Ontario CAN Monday and Tuesday, turning winds \neast and keeping temperatures cooler. \n\nUpper ridging will return to MI mid week, with another system \nriding up and over it towards the Great Lakes region. WAA will \nreturn mild temperatures to northern MI for mid week. Global \ndeterministic guidance hints at the system returning more \nwidespread rain chances to northern MI for late in the workweek,\nhowever ensembles continue to remain spread on rain amounts and\ncoverage. Temperatures continue to be mild through the end of \nthe month.",
